; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; tscaly_types — our half of the CHECKER yardstick (slice 46; answering since 47).
;
; Dumps our check of one file in the format tests/oracle/types.go produces:
;
;   C <pos> <end> <code>                 one line per checker diagnostic
;   T <kind> <pos> <end> <typeString>    one line per node the type walk asks about
;
; ★★★ THE YARDSTICK SHIPPED BEFORE THE PHASE IT MEASURES, which is the fourth time
; this suite has done that (§3.5bd for jsdoc, §3.5bv for the binder) and the same
; reason each time: a TYPE is not in the tree and not on a symbol. It is computed on
; demand, memoised inside the checker and never written back, so with four artifacts
; a port that computes no types and a port that computes wrong ones compare exactly
; equal, in both directions. Slice 46 committed the artifact with every unit
; reporting `UNPORTED 0 checker 0`; slice 47 gave it a checker, and the report is
; now the checker's own — one arm at a time, which is what makes the unported column
; the work list (tests/triage.py groups it by tag).
;
; ★★ THE LAYERING IS THE SAME AS THE BINDER'S, and the order matters: a parse's own
; unported report comes first, then the bind's, and only then the check's. A tree
; built past a construct the parser could not scan has meaningless spans, and a
; check over an incomplete bind would report the bind's gap as the checker's.
;
; ★ SINCE SLICE 47 THE CHECK IS A THIRD STEP rather than a marker: the report is
; asked of `Checker.unported_at` the way the bind's is asked of the binder, and the
; dump is built by `TypeDump` inside the package. This program stays three steps —
; parse, bind, check — and grows no checker of its own: a generic instance handed to
; a program root reads back as garbage, which is why every dumper asks for
; everything through statics.
;
; ★★★ THE PROGRAM THE REFERENCE CHECKS AGAINST IS A STUB, AND OURS WILL HAVE TO BE
; THE SAME STUB. tests/oracle/types.go builds no real program: it answers every
; module-resolution question with nothing and fixes the lib set to lib.es5.d.ts,
; priced there at a measured 3 % diagnostic delta against the reference's default
; lib closure. So when this side starts answering, it inherits three obligations —
; the same lib file, no module resolution, and the per-case `@target`/`@lib`
; directives ignored — and the first one of them is where the RBMM question of
; §3.11 lands: the lib's tree has to outlive a per-unit region without being copied
; into it.

package scaly 0.1.0
package tscaly 0.1.0

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.io.File
use scaly.containers.String
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.tspath.TsPath
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.binder.Binder
use tscaly.checker.Checker
use tscaly.TypeDump.TypeDump

; getcwd(3), for the reason tscaly_symbols states at its own declaration: the
; reference asks the same question in the same place, and tspath computes over
; strings while the caller supplies them.
use scaly.io.Console

function getcwd(buf: pointer[char], size: size_t) returns pointer[char] extern

function main(argc: int, argv: pointer[pointer[char]]) returns int
{
    ; ★ THREE MODES, and the two flagged ones are INSTRUMENTS rather than dumps.
    ; Both exist because the fifth yardstick's two sections gate each other, so
    ; each one asks about the half the other hides:
    ;
    ;   --walk    the node LIST the type walk would ask about (`W kind pos end`),
    ;             no types, no check — tests/walkcheck.sh diffs it against the
    ;             reference's own T lines. Without it the walk's exclusions are
    ;             transcribed code no control can turn red in 1 030 of 1 040 units.
    ;   --diags   the C section ALONE, printed whether or not the check reported —
    ;             so the grammar checks, the span grammarErrorOnFirstToken computes
    ;             and the diagnostic SORT are measured on the units whose dump is
    ;             suppressed. tests/diagcheck.sh asserts the lines are a
    ;             SUBSEQUENCE of the reference's.
    ; ★★★ AND A FOURTH, `--sections`, WHICH IS THE OTHER THREE IN ONE PROCESS.
    ; diagcheck and walkcheck each spawned this program once per unit — 35 784
    ; processes on stage 2, 216 s — to recompute a parse and a bind that run.sh had
    ; just performed in the same minute. This mode prints all three answers,
    ; separated, so ONE invocation feeds all three readers.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT A FOURTH ANSWER: each section is produced by the SAME call
    ; sequence on a FRESH Checker that its own mode uses, in that mode's own order
    ; — the walk BEFORE any check (its mode returns before `get_diagnostics`), then
    ; the diags, then the dump. Sharing one checker across the three would be the
    ; cheaper thing and the wrong one: `write_walk` may memoise, and a walk taken
    ; after a check is a different question from the one walkcheck asks. What is
    ; shared is the process, the read, the PARSE and the BIND — the parts that are
    ; identical in all three by construction.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND EVERY LINE THIS MODE PRINTS GOES THROUGH `Console.println`, NEVER
    ; THROUGH THE PRELUDE'S `print` — THE TWO ARE DIFFERENT STREAMS TO THE SAME FD.
    ; `print` is the prelude's, which calls `puts`, i.e. C stdio and its BUFFER;
    ; `Console.print` is `Io.write_all(1, …)`, a raw write. Mix them and stdio's
    ; buffer flushes at exit, so every `print` line lands AFTER everything the
    ; other stream already wrote. Measured the first time this mode ran: 97 of
    ; 1 123 units had the diags section's C line sitting inside the WALK section
    ; and the separators at the end. ★It is latent in the other three modes only
    ; because none of them emits through both — the dump path prints UNPORTED
    ; exactly when `emit` returned before its `Console.print`. This mode is the
    ; first to need both, and the rule is: one stream per program run.
    ;
    ; ★ The four early exits below (`File.exists`, an unported parse, a null tree,
    ; an unported bind) are MODE-INDEPENDENT — every mode prints the same line and
    ; returns — so in this mode that one line becomes all three sections, which is
    ; exactly what the three separate processes would each have printed.
    var walk_only false
    var diags_only false
    var stops_only false
    var flow_only false
    var unions_only false
    var members_only false
    var sections false
    var path_arg 1
    if argc = 3
    {
        let flag String(*(argv + 1))
        if flag.equals(String("--walk"))
        {
            set walk_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        if flag.equals(String("--diags"))
        {
            set diags_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        ; ★ THE STOP HISTOGRAM'S FLAG (slice 75). It is deliberately NOT part of
        ; `--sections`: that mode exists to spare the loop a process where the parse
        ; and the bind have already been paid for, and this instrument is not run
        ; per slice — adding it there would make every run.sh write a third sidecar
        ; for the sake of a question asked once a chapter.
        if flag.equals(String("--stops"))
        {
            set stops_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        ; ★ THE MEMBER PIN'S FLAG (slice 86), out of `--sections` for the reason
        ; `--stops` is out of it: it is a control-battery instrument, asked once a
        ; chapter, and a fourth sidecar per run.sh would be paid by every run.
        if flag.equals(String("--members"))
        {
            set members_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        ; ★ THE FLOW PIN'S FLAG (slice 93), out of `--sections` for `--members`'
        ; reason.
        if flag.equals(String("--flow"))
        {
            set flow_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        ; ★ THE UNION PIN'S FLAG (slice 94), out of `--sections` for `--flow`'s
        ; reason.
        if flag.equals(String("--unions"))
        {
            set unions_only: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
        if flag.equals(String("--sections"))
        {
            set sections: true
            set path_arg: 2
        }
    }
    if walk_only = false
    {
        if diags_only = false
        {
            if stops_only = false
            {
                if members_only = false
                {
                    if flow_only = false
                    {
                        if unions_only = false
                        {
                            if sections = false
                            {
                                if argc <> 2
                                {
                                    print "usage: tscaly_types [--walk|--diags|--stops|--members|--flow|--unions|--sections] <file>"
                                    return 2
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    let arg String(*(argv + path_arg))
    if File.exists(arg) = false
    {
        print "cannot read `arg`"
        return 1
    }

    let text File.read_to_string(arg)

    ; The run region and its small anchor: §3.12's rule, because the source text of
    ; a large unit is an OVERSIZED allocation and Page.get() of an address inside
    ; one answers a page whose first allocation trips the oversized-page trap.
    let anchor String("tscaly")
    let host Page.get(anchor.get_buffer() as pointer[void])

    let cwd_cap 4096
    let cwd_buf host.allocate(cwd_cap as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
    if getcwd(cwd_buf, cwd_cap as size_t) = null
    {
        print "cannot read the working directory"
        return 1
    }
    var cwd_len 0
    while (*(cwd_buf + cwd_len) as int) <> 0
        set cwd_len: cwd_len + 1

    var name_data null as pointer[char]
    let name_len TsPath.normalized_absolute_path(host, arg.get_buffer() as pointer[char], arg.get_length() as int, cwd_buf, cwd_len, &name_data)
    let path String(name_data as pointer[const_char], name_len as size_t)

    var unported 0 - 1
    var unported_tag null as pointer[const_char]
    var unported_detail 0
    let script_kind Parser.supported_script_kind(path)
    ; ★ parse_ex rather than parse (slice 78): the checker's JSDoc pass reads the
    ; parser's own eager-JSDoc table, so the parser has to survive the parse here
    ; for the same reason the JSDoc yardstick needed it in slice 21.
    let p Parser.parse_ex(host, path, text, script_kind, &unported, &unported_tag, &unported_detail)
    let file Parser.source_file_of(p)

    if unported >= 0
    {
        if sections
        {
            let m String("UNPORTED `unported` `unported_tag` `unported_detail`")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION diags")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION dump")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            return 0
        }
        print "UNPORTED `unported` `unported_tag` `unported_detail`"
        return 0
    }

    if file = null
    {
        if sections
        {
            Console.println("UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0")
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION diags")
            Console.println("UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0")
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION dump")
            Console.println("UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0")
            return 0
        }
        print "UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0"
        return 0
    }

    let is_external Parser.external_module_indicator(file, AstNode.statements_of(file))
    let b Binder.bind_source_file(host, file, text, path, is_external)

    if Binder.unported_at(b) >= 0
    {
        if sections
        {
            let m String("UNPORTED `Binder.unported_at(b)` `Binder.unported_tag_at(b)` `Binder.unported_detail_at(b)`")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION diags")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION dump")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
            return 0
        }
        print "UNPORTED `Binder.unported_at(b)` `Binder.unported_tag_at(b)` `Binder.unported_detail_at(b)`"
        return 0
    }

    ; The check, and the dump. TypeDump answers false when either half met
    ; something this slice does not implement, and then the checker's own report is
    ; the unit's whole answer — the same arrangement the parse and the bind use two
    ; steps up.
    if sections
    {
        let cw Checker.create(host, file, path, b, p)
        TypeDump.write_walk(host, cw, file)
        Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION diags")
        let cd Checker.create(host, file, path, b, p)
        cd.get_diagnostics()
        TypeDump.write_diags(host, cd, file)
        Console.println("==== TSCALY-SECTION dump")
        let cs Checker.create(host, file, path, b, p)
        cs.get_diagnostics()
        if TypeDump.write(host, cs, file) = false
        {
            let m String("UNPORTED `Checker.unported_at(cs)` `Checker.unported_tag_at(cs)` `Checker.unported_detail_at(cs)`")
            Console.println(m.to_c_string())
        }
        return 0
    }

    let c Checker.create(host, file, path, b, p)
    if walk_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_walk(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    c.get_diagnostics()
    if diags_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_diags(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    ; ★ The check has to RUN before the log can be read — the log is what the check
    ; left behind — which is why this sits after get_diagnostics and not beside the
    ; walk, whose caller deliberately does not run it.
    if stops_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_stops(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    if members_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_members(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    if flow_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_flow(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    if unions_only
    {
        TypeDump.write_unions(host, c, file)
        return 0
    }
    if TypeDump.write(host, c, file) = false
    {
        print "UNPORTED `Checker.unported_at(c)` `Checker.unported_tag_at(c)` `Checker.unported_detail_at(c)`"
        return 0
    }
    0
}
